# Roster — which model runs which persona, and at what effort

One copy. Every `TASK.md` names a persona from this file; if a task and this file disagree,
**this file wins and one of them needs fixing.**

A persona is a description of what someone is responsible for. It is not a capability, and
writing one does not confer one. **The model and the effort are what the persona is actually
made of** — so they are declared here, per persona, with the reason, and the reason is the part
that survives a model rename.

---

## 0 · The floor, and why there is one

**Effort is never below `high`.** Not on recon, not on a sweep, not on "just a count."

The tempting saving is on the cheap-looking work, and that is exactly where it has cost most.
Two examples from this method's own history, both on tasks that looked mechanical:

- A recon pass reported `app/src/entities/*.js` as dead modules. They were live re-export shims
  with **34 importers**; deleting them would have broken the build. A re-export does not look
  like usage textually, and the pass that missed it was the cheapest one in the round.
- A capability was recorded **CONFIRMED** because its description string appeared in a compiled
  binary. The tool could not do the thing. Reading a string is not calling a function, and
  noticing the difference is not free.

Neither was a knowledge failure. Both were attention failures on work that had been priced as
routine. **The tier buys attention, and attention is what these tasks needed.**

`low` and `medium` do not appear in this file. If a job genuinely does not deserve `high`, it
probably does not deserve an agent — write the shell command instead.

**The floor is about misclassification, not about attention being purchasable.** Both failures above
happened because the task was *priced* as routine, not because the model was set too low for what it
was told to do. A floor is the cheap insurance against a classification you got wrong; it is not a
claim that spending more always buys more.

> **And it is not free — this is the honest cost.** Anthropic's own guidance for `claude-opus-5` says
> `low` and `medium` "punch well above their weight," and that at higher effort on routine work the
> model can gather context and deliberate beyond what the task needs. So a floor of `high` buys
> insurance against misclassification and pays for it in tokens on the tasks that were genuinely
> simple. That trade is deliberate here — a wrong "21 of 49" costs more than the tokens — but a plan
> that has *measured* its own recon accuracy has earned the right to lower it. Measure, then move it.

